Q: Is retrieving the row before calling put() the only way to update a single field in GridDB? A: <p>In the GridDB Java Container API, updates are row-based, not field-based.</p>
<p>So for a normal <code>Collection</code> row update, the usual approach is:</p>
<pre><code>Device device = container.get(&quot;device-101&quot;);

if (device != null) {
    device.setStatus(&quot;ACTIVE&quot;);
    container.put(device);
}
</code></pre>
<p><code>put()</code> updates the existing row when the same row key already exists. It does not update only the changed field; it writes the row object back.</p>
<p>Do not create a new <code>Device</code> object with only the row key and <code>status</code> set unless you intentionally want the other columns to be replaced by default/null values. GridDB treats the row object as the row content, not as a patch.</p>
<p>If you need concurrency safety, use manual commit mode and lock the row/search result before updating, then commit.</p>
<p>There is one important distinction: GridDB SQL supports <code>UPDATE table SET column = ... WHERE ...</code>, but that is the SQL interface, not the normal Java Container <code>get()</code>/<code>put()</code> API workflow.</p>

Q: How do you make an input take two scripts? A: <p>Both scripts define the same global variables/functions. When you load the second script, it smashes the definitions of the first script.</p>
<p>You need each to have different names.</p>
<pre><code>var myStringDict1 = { ... }
function testMultipleStrings1() { ... }
</code></pre>
<p>and</p>
<pre><code>var myStringDict2 = { ... }
function testMultipleStrings2() { ... }
</code></pre>
<p>That also means you need to change the <code>oninput=&quot;...&quot;</code>. If you have a single <code>oninput=&quot;...&quot;</code> and still want two separate scripts, then you need a different approach. For example, you could add to the map instead of overwriting it in the second script. Here is how you could do it:</p>
<pre><code>myStringDict[&quot;exampletwo&quot;] = &quot;example.org&quot;
myStringDict[&quot;othertexttwo&quot;] = &quot;othersitehere&quot;
</code></pre>
<p>And in that case you do not need to redefine the <code>testMultipleStrings()</code> function.</p>
